module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.container_status_read_as_a_claim_about_its_members

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data container_status_read_as_a_claim_about_its_members: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "container_status_read_as_a_claim_about_its_members" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**container status read as a claim about its members** (an aggregate field on a CONTAINER is read as though it asserted something about the container's MEMBERS. The field is not stale, not censored and not lying: it is answering a question about the container, faithfully, and the reader asked a question about a member. Every conclusion drawn is then true of the wrapper and false of the contents. ",

    "**THE BOUNDARY AGAINST `instrument_output_read_as_subject_content`, which is the nearest row and has a different repair.** There the completeness of a SET is a property of the REPORTING TOOL rather than of the subject, and the repair is to bind the report to a subject-side enumeration. Here nothing is incomplete -- the container reports its own state correctly and totally -- and the repair is to READ THE MEMBER COLLECTION INSTEAD, which is a different call, not a wider one. Widening the container query returns more containers, all equally silent about members. ",

    "**SPECIMEN WITH A RECEIPT, measured 2026-09-04 on GitHub Actions run 33853722488 (gunbc#10382), and it is a specimen precisely because THREE sessions read one run within an hour and all three concluded wrongly.** A cancel was REQUESTED from a session at 08:47Z -- a client action, not a run event, and the only non-run timestamp in this paragraph. Read per job via `/actions/runs/33853722488/jobs`: five jobs cancelled between 08:51:23Z and 08:52:16Z, one skipped, and `witnesses` never scheduled. The run's own status stayed `queued` for the next twenty-one minutes and its final conclusion is `cancelled`, while the job that actually decided its completion has conclusion `failure` -- it acquired a runner at 09:13:18Z, ran for FOUR SECONDS, and failed at 09:13:22Z. So the container says cancelled and a member says failure; the container says queued and five of seven members are already terminal. Both container answers are correct about the container. ",

    "**THE THREE WRONG READINGS, kept because the pattern is in how they failed rather than in that they failed.** (1) `the cancel is queued behind the thing it is cancelling` -- inferred from the run status, when the cancel had already succeeded on five members including three that had acquired named runners. (2) `the aggregate follows its laggard, so a mostly-cancelled run READS as live` -- right about the mechanism, wrong about the harm: the run was not misreporting, it was genuinely unfinished. (3) `a run status is not monotone` -- an in_progress -> queued transition read from the run LISTING, never reproduced against the run resource, and withdrawn. Each reading was a different wrong theory about ONE MISSING JOB QUERY. ",

    "**THE COST IS REAL AND IS NOT A REPORTING ARTIFACT, which is the distinction the first two readings fused.** `.github/workflows/witnesses.yml` sets `cancel-in-progress: false` and keys the concurrency group per pull request, so the live-head run for the same PR could not register a single check while this one stood. TWO INTERVALS ARE MEASURABLE HERE AND THEY ARE DIFFERENT SUBJECTS, which is worth stating because they coincide in this specimen and diverge in the general case: DEAD TIME UNDER HOLD, last member ends to next member starts, 08:52:16Z -> 09:13:18Z = 1262s, during which ZERO members ran; and GROUP HOLD, last member ends to container settles, 08:52:16Z -> 09:13:22Z = 1266s, during which the group was UNAVAILABLE TO SUCCESSORS. They differ by four seconds here and by the laggard's whole runtime in general. Dead time prices WASTED CAPACITY; group hold prices BLOCKED SUCCESSORS, and a starvation question is always the second one. The laggard's own history is the sharpest datum: `witnesses` sat queued 2,601 seconds from run creation, acquired srv3-01, ran four seconds and failed -- forty-three minutes of group occupancy purchasing four seconds of work and no verdict. THE QUANTITY NOT TO MEASURE, because it is the one a reader reaches for first: lag between the last member transition and the container's resolution is ZERO BY CONSTRUCTION, since the laggard's completion IS the container's completion. It will always return ~0 and always tell you nothing. ",

    "**SPECIMEN TWO, measured by warm-seal-35 and re-measured here at 09:27Z on a four-PR table, and it is what turns this from a restatement into a generalisation: THE CONTAINER IS A PULL REQUEST, NOT A WORKFLOW RUN.** All four rows read live and open unless marked: gunbc#10365 OPEN base=session/eager-otter-105 check_runs=0 CLEAN; gunbc#10387 OPEN base=main check_runs=0 BLOCKED (this row's own PR); gunbc#10300 OPEN base=main check_runs=6 BLOCKED; gunbc#10333 MERGED base=main check_runs=14 UNKNOWN. CLEAN is a CORRECT AND TOTAL statement about the container -- nothing is currently BLOCKING the merge, and with no members registered nothing blocks. The reader wants `did the checks pass`, a fact about MEMBERS. A session came within one query of merging an entirely unevaluated head on #10365, stopped only by counting check-runs beside a control. **THE TABLE SAYS THE SILENCE RUNS BOTH WAYS, AND THE SECOND DIRECTION IS THE ONE THAT HIDES.** Hold the members at ZERO and vary the base: #10365 and #10387 have identical empty member sets, are both open, and read CLEAN and BLOCKED respectively -- the verdict flips on the base and its protection while the members stay fixed, so it is not merely silent about members, it varies with something else entirely. Now hold the base at main and vary the members: #10387 has ZERO check-runs and #10300 has SIX, and BOTH READ BLOCKED -- the verdict is constant across a state where no member exists and a state where six exist and are pending. Neither direction carries information about members. The CLEAN case is the dangerous one because it invites a merge; the BLOCKED case is the quiet one, because two materially different states are reported in the same word and nobody investigates a BLOCKED. **AND THE CONTROL IN THIS SPECIMEN DECAYED WHILE IT WAS BEING WRITTEN, WHICH IS THE ROW'S OWN CLASS ONE LEVEL UP.** #10333 was first cited as the check_runs=14 / UNKNOWN contrast; it had already MERGED, so its UNKNOWN is driven by LIFECYCLE, a third variable, and the verdict half of that pair isolated nothing. Its nonzero check_runs still serves as a control for the QUERY, which is all it is now used for. A specimen whose control silently changes subject is the same defect the row files. ",

    "**SPECIMEN THREE, also warm-seal-35, same container type but a DIFFERENT FIELD, which shows the class is not a property of one endpoint.** A falling series of `actions/runs?status=in_progress` was published as evidence that the CI pool was losing capacity. A peer counted `runner_name` over in-progress JOBS instead: 38 concurrent across srv1-srv4, all four serving. `in_progress` counts RUNS, and a run holds that status until its LAST member resolves -- so a saturated pool doing SLOWER work produces exactly the same falling series as a shrinking one, and the container-level series fits both stories while discriminating neither. ",

    "**SPECIMEN FOUR, AND IT IS THE ONE THAT SHOWS THE RULE IS NOT ENOUGH: the two authors of this row committed the class again, in one exchange, hours after filing it.** A run was cancelled; the cancel was claimed complete from the POST return. The second reader corrected that from the RUN field -- status queued, conclusion null -- and concluded the cancel had not taken. The truth was one level below both: at job grain, SIX OF SEVEN JOBS WERE TERMINAL in each of two runs, and the only survivor was the aggregating job, unscheduled. The cancel had taken everywhere it could. Three stops, three grains, and the correct one was reached last. ",

    "**WHY THE RULE FAILED TO FIRE ON ITS OWN AUTHORS, in the second author's own account: the grain rule had been written down that morning as `wait on the JOB set, never on the run reaching a status` -- AND FILED UNDER A VERB INSTEAD OF UNDER A SUBJECT.** It was then applied only to WAITING. It is not about waiting. It governs every question asked of a container -- cancelled, started, finished, held, superseded -- because in each one the answer is a fact about MEMBERS, and the container field does not own it. A rule indexed by the verb of the question it was discovered under will not be retrieved for the next verb, and the next verb is where it is needed. ",

    "**THE SUFFICIENT CLAIM, STATED WITHOUT AN AGGREGATION LAW NOBODY MEASURED: A CONTAINER FIELD DOES NOT OWN PREDICATES OVER ITS MEMBER POPULATION.** That forbids every coercion this row is about while leaving the container's computation rule OPAQUE, which is the stronger position: it cannot be falsified by an upstream change to a scheduler nobody here controls. An earlier draft of this row asserted that the container reports its LEAST-ADVANCED member -- an aggregation law licensed by nothing that was measured. Three samples were all consistent with it and none was a differential that varied ONE member's state while fixing the rest, which is the only shape that could have refused it. That is this roster's `population_selector_that_cannot_admit_a_counterexample` committed inside the row filing its neighbour, and it is withdrawn here rather than shipped. **WHAT IS VERIFIABLE IS OURS, NOT UPSTREAM'S**: in this repository's emitted workflow the aggregating job declares `needs: [required-witnesses-build, required-witnesses-floor]`, so it is last by OUR topology, and a run cannot be terminal before its last job is. That is a fact about bytes this repository generates. The operative consequence survives on insufficiency alone: for this workflow the run-level field is never the right read. Three sightings in nine hours are one object, not a flaky scheduler -- a run queued twenty-one minutes with six jobs terminal, and two more with the identical split. Observed from the healthy direction on the same day: a PR sat eighty-plus minutes with six jobs green and the aggregator queued while the fleet ran twenty-eight concurrent jobs across four servers. For this workflow the run-level field is NEVER the right read. ",

    "**THE MOST DANGEROUS PART IS THAT THE OPERATIVE ADVICE SURVIVED THE WRONG JUSTIFICATION.** `the group is still held` was true under both readings -- a cancel that succeeds completely still does not release the slot while one member stays unscheduled -- so the practical guidance never changed and nothing in the outcome flagged that its reasoning was false. Right answer, wrong instrument, no signal. That is the state in which a container-level read survives indefinitely: it is not producing visibly wrong advice, it is producing correct advice for a reason that will fail silently the first time the two diverge. ",

    "**RECOGNITION RULE: name the subject of the field before drawing the conclusion.** If the sentence you are about to write says `the run`, `the batch`, `the suite`, `the deployment` or `the transaction`, and the fact you need is about a job, an item, a test, an instance or a statement, you are one query short. THE MEASURED CORPUS IS THREE INSTANCES IN ONE SYSTEM, NOT FIVE DOMAINS: batch/item, suite/test, deployment/instance and transaction/statement are ILLUSTRATIONS OF THE SHAPE and carry no receipt here. They are named so a reader recognises the shape elsewhere, not as evidence it has been observed there -- an unmeasured analogy sitting beside real receipts borrows their credibility, and the next reader cannot tell which is which. The tell is that the conclusion is about a plural and the evidence is about a singular. The check is cheap and it is the whole repair: fetch the member collection and count it. ",

    "**AND A COROLLARY THAT COST MORE THAN THE ORIGINAL ERROR: a count printed beneath the member table it summarises is not evidence, it is a second claim.** In this specimen the seven-row job table was pasted correctly and the sentence under it said `six of seven cancelled` when the table shows five; it was then relayed into a downstream worker's brief and only caught when that worker added the rows up. A member listing does not immunise the summary standing next to it -- the arithmetic is the check, and nobody ran it for two hops. ",

    "**RUNG: 1 (mitigatable).** The class is nameable and, once found, held open by the habit of fetching the member collection. **CEILING: 2 (mechanically preventable) for our own consumers, not higher:** where the container is an EXTERNAL system's API the grain is outside the modeled guarantee, so no constructor of ours can make the confusion unwritable; what is reachable is a typed accessor per external container that has no member-count-shaped field at all, forcing the member query. **NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, a capability and not an artifact: a modeled external-container binding whose read surface makes THE MEMBER QUERY THE ONLY PATH TO A MEMBER FACT.** Omitting aggregates that merely LOOK numeric is not sufficient and specimen two is why: `BLOCKED` is not member-count-shaped in any way, and it is read as a member fact regardless. The trigger is satisfied only when no container-level field can be reached where a member fact is what the consumer needs -- so a consumer cannot spell the aggregate-about-members sentence in the first place. ",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
